$WW,1$$FG,5,0$Go Cross-Compilation Guide for ZealOS$FG$

$TR,"Overview"$
ZealOS supports running Go programs through cross-compilation.
Since the full Go compiler requires significant system resources,
we recommend compiling Go programs on a host system (Linux, macOS,
or Windows) and then running the resulting binaries on ZealOS.

$TR,"Requirements"$
- Host system with Go 1.18 or later installed
- ZealOS with the POSIX layer loaded

$TR,"Cross-Compilation Steps"$

$FG,2$1. On your host system (Linux/macOS/Windows):$FG$

   For static Linux binary (recommended):
   $FG,4$GOOS=linux GOARCH=amd64 CGO_ENABLED=0 go build -o myprogram myprogram.go$FG$

   This produces a statically-linked ELF binary that ZealOS can load.

$FG,2$2. Transfer the binary to ZealOS:$FG$

   Copy the binary to your ZealOS disk, for example:
   - Mount the ZealOS disk on your host
   - Copy to /Home/POSIX/go/bin/ or any accessible location

$FG,2$3. On ZealOS, run the binary:$FG$

   $FG,4$go run /path/to/myprogram$FG$

   Or directly through the ELF loader:
   $FG,4$ELFRun("/path/to/myprogram", argc, argv);$FG$

$TR,"Example Programs"$

$FG,2$Hello World (hello.go):$FG$
$FG,4$
package main

import "fmt"

func main() {
    fmt.Println("Hello from Go on ZealOS!")
}
$FG$

Compile: $FG,4$GOOS=linux GOARCH=amd64 CGO_ENABLED=0 go build -o hello hello.go$FG$

$FG,2$Goroutines (goroutines.go):$FG$
$FG,4$
package main

import (
    "fmt"
    "time"
)

func worker(id int) {
    fmt.Printf("Worker %d starting\n", id)
    time.Sleep(time.Second)
    fmt.Printf("Worker %d done\n", id)
}

func main() {
    for i := 1; i <= 5; i++ {
        go worker(i)
    }
    time.Sleep(2 * time.Second)
    fmt.Println("All workers complete")
}
$FG$

$TR,"Supported Features"$

$FG,2$Working:$FG$
- Goroutines (cooperative scheduling)
- Channels
- Standard library (most packages)
- File I/O
- Networking (with POSIX layer)
- Time functions
- Synchronization primitives

$FG,2$Limitations:$FG$
- CGO is not supported (CGO_ENABLED=0 required)
- Some syscalls may behave differently
- Memory mapped I/O simplified
- Signal handling simplified
- Single-threaded execution (GOMAXPROCS=1)

$TR,"Troubleshooting"$

$FG,2$"exec format error":$FG$
   Ensure you compiled with GOOS=linux GOARCH=amd64

$FG,2$"syscall not implemented":$FG$
   Some advanced syscalls are not available. Try:
   - Using simpler alternatives in your code
   - Checking if the syscall is in GoSyscall.ZC

$FG,2$"out of memory":$FG$
   Go programs can be memory-intensive. Ensure enough
   free memory on ZealOS before running.

$FG,2$Program hangs:$FG$
   Check for blocking syscalls or infinite loops.
   ZealOS uses cooperative multitasking - ensure your
   program yields occasionally.

$TR,"Advanced: Adding New Syscalls"$

If your Go program needs a syscall that's not implemented:

1. Find the syscall number in GoSyscall.ZC
2. Add a case handler that maps to your POSIX function
3. Test with a simple Go program that uses that syscall

Example adding a new syscall:
$FG,4$
case SYS_NEWSYSCALL:
    ret = my_posix_function(a1, a2, a3);
    break;
$FG$

$TR,"Performance Tips"$

- Use CGO_ENABLED=0 for smallest, most compatible binaries
- Avoid excessive goroutine creation
- Use buffered channels where possible
- Minimize file I/O operations
- Consider GOMAXPROCS=1 behavior in your design

$TR,"Files"$

$FG,4$/Home/POSIX/go/Go.HH$FG$ - Go runtime definitions
$FG,4$/Home/POSIX/go/Futex.ZC$FG$ - Futex synchronization
$FG,4$/Home/POSIX/go/runtime/GoRuntime.ZC$FG$ - M-P-G scheduler
$FG,4$/Home/POSIX/go/runtime/GoSyscall.ZC$FG$ - Syscall implementations
$FG,4$/Home/POSIX/go/MakeGo.ZC$FG$ - Main loader
